	(text "HOUSEKEEPING RAIL"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 299.7200 81.2800 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.8000 1.8000)
				(bold yes)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "c6740439-0b18-7ef8-906e-0820c5d3b668")
	)
	(text_box "ONE SHARED RAIL FOR EVERY THRESHOLD-SETTING GATE.\n+3V3_A feeds all 14 squarers, all 26 flip-flops, all 12 Schmitts and the whole\nhelper fan-out.  A gate's switching threshold moves with its supply, so on a\nshared rail every channel's threshold moves TOGETHER - common mode, and it\ncancels in the differences we actually compute BETWEEN CHANNELS OF EQUAL SLEW.\nRound 28: dt = dV / slew, and the inventory's slews span 23 mV/ns (FE-5680A,\n0.72 Vpp sine) to ~1600 mV/ns (ZED-F9T CMOS), so 1 mV of common rail shift is 44\nps on one and 0.6 ps on the other, and 43 ps of it does NOT cancel between them.\nThat residual is the analog floor for the sine sources and the reason the board\nis heated and the rail is a 7 uV LDO.  Fourteen separate regulators would still\nbe worse: they would add fourteen independent wanders on top."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.2400 144.7800 0)
		(size 121.9200 31.7500)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4000 1.4000)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "6961e0e3-6dee-c4eb-d29d-7b9c82586657")
	)
	(text_box "WHY A SECOND RAIL AT ALL.\n+3V3_D carries only the Si5351, the TCXO and the I2C housekeeping parts.  None\nof them sets a comparison threshold, and they draw the lumpiest current on the\nboard. Splitting them off halves the dissipation in each SOT-23 package - about\n120 mW and 50 mW instead of 170 mW in one - which matters on a board whose whole\npurpose is thermal stability."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 144.7800 144.7800 0)
		(size 121.9200 17.7800)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4000 1.4000)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "9cff00ab-ad82-0407-a8e9-d18d8bfd6dcb")
	)
	(text_box "WHY THE TPS7A2033.\n7 uV RMS output noise (10 Hz - 100 kHz, 300 mA; 10 uV at 1 mA), 300 mA, and 52\n000 in stock at JLCPCB.  The obvious alternative, the ADP151, is 9 uV and 200 mA\nand is not stocked there at all - only re-marked house-brand parts are.\nThe low noise needs NO external part: the reference filter is a 0.1 Hz first-\norder RC on the die, and the soft-start is internal too.  Pin 4 of the SOT-23-5\nis a true no-connect.  Leave it open."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 274.3200 144.7800 0)
		(size 121.9200 21.5900)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4000 1.4000)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "60fdedfb-ab17-f237-a15e-15bdb65c4177")
	)
	(text_box "STUDENT-PROOFING THE 5 V INPUT.\nBench supplies get set wrong.  Three things, in order: each source has its own\nSERIES SCHOTTKY, so reversed leads conduct nothing and the two sources cannot\nback-feed each other; a 500 mA PPTC, which RESETS ITSELF - nobody has to find\nthe bag of spare fuses; and a 5.6 V 5 W Zener, which is the part that saves the\nboard if 12 V lands on the 5 V posts.\nThe Zener is what makes the fuse work: it clamps the rail near 6.1 V - under the\nTPS7A2033's 6.5 V absolute maximum - and draws the amp that trips the PPTC in\nabout 100 ms.  It is the deliberate sacrificial part, and it is a 16-cent SMB on\nthe board edge.\nWhat is NOT protected: sustained reverse voltage beyond the SS34's 40 V, and\nanything applied directly to an SMA or the header pins.\nNEVER RUN OFF THE BENCH POSTS WITH THE PYNQ UNPOWERED.  PYNQ-Z2 user manual\ntable 15: an unpowered Zynq I/O pin is absolute-max 0.55 V.  This board would\nthen drive 3.3 V into all 13 beats, 12 pps returns and the helper clock.  The\nPYNQ's own 200 R series resistors hold each one to about 14 mA, so it survives a\nmistake, but it is out of spec every second it lasts.  The bench posts are for\npowering the board when the PYNQ is ALSO on - to take the 3.3 V rails off the\nhost - not for running it alone."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 403.8600 144.7800 0)
		(size 121.9200 53.3400)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 220 220 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4000 1.4000)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "526a53dd-7b7c-22ee-339e-70e9d04b18bd")
	)
	(text_box "CURRENT BUDGET, ~100 mA TOTAL.\n14 x 74LVC1GU04 self-biased in the linear region ~3 mA each = 42 mA;  26 x\n74LVC1G80 at 10 MHz ~1 mA each = 26 mA;  5 x 74LVC1G34 fan-out ~2 mA each = 10\nmA; 12 x 74LVC1G17 at 1 Hz ~ 1 mA total;  Si5351 ~25 mA.  The linear-region\ncurrent of a self-biased unbuffered inverter is not a datasheet number - measure\nit on the first board."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 403.8600 100.3300 0)
		(size 121.9200 17.7800)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4000 1.4000)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "9aacb817-34df-d296-bdaf-9681f341a0fe")
	)
